measures.retrofit_conditions:
Mann recommends upgrading the DHW system through a comprehensive modernization approach, including:

• Replacement of the failed DHW boiler with a new high-efficiency condensing DHW boiler of similar capacity;
• Installation of two (2) additional RBI glass-lined water heaters (matching existing units) to increase total storage capacity;
• Full repiping of the DHW boiler room to optimize system hydraulic configuration and temperature control;
• Decommissioning and removal (or permanent isolation) of the existing aging large-volume storage tank.

Under the proposed configuration, the new condensing DHW boiler would be configured to accept low return-water temperatures, allowing direct introduction of cold domestic water into the system to promote condensing operation and maximize thermal efficiency.

The remaining existing boiler may continue to operate under a higher return-water temperature regime, supplied from the storage tank loop (typically 135–140°F), with appropriate mixing control (e.g., via a mixing valve from the condensing boiler supply) to maintain stable and safe return-water temperatures.

By increasing distributed storage capacity through additional glass-lined tanks rather than relying on a single aging vessel, the system achieves improved redundancy, faster recovery, and more stable DHW delivery under peak demand conditions.

This configuration also aligns system operation with proper boiler application:
– condensing boiler operating in low-temperature return conditions for high efficiency;
– non-condensing boiler operating in protected higher-temperature range to avoid heat exchanger damage.

Together, these upgrades significantly improve system reliability, resolve hot water shortage issues, and reduce long-term maintenance risks associated with aging equipment.

